{"name":"Fever","hex":"#ea4c4c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"fever","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A higher than normal body temperature of a person (or, generally, a mammal), usually caused by disease.","Any of various diseases."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To put into a fever; to affect with fever.","To become fevered."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fever"}],"entries":[{"title":"Fever","blocks":[{"text":"Fever or pyrexia in humans is a symptom of an anti-infection defense mechanism that appears with body temperature exceeding the normal range caused by an increase in the body's temperature set point in the hypothalamus. There is no single agreed-upon upper limit for normal temperature: sources use values ranging between 37.2 and 38.3 °C in humans."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Symptoms-fever.jpg/330px-Symptoms-fever.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fever"},{"title":"Fever (Kylie Minogue album)","blocks":[{"text":"Fever is the eighth studio album by Australian singer Kylie Minogue. It was released on 1 October 2001, by Parlophone. Minogue worked with writers and producers such as Cathy Dennis, Rob Davis, Richard Stannard, Julian Gallagher, TommyD, Tom Nichols, Pascal Gabriel and others to create a disco and Europop-influenced dance-pop and nu-disco record. Other musical influences of the album range from synth-pop to club music."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/a/a9/Kylie_Minogue_-_Fever.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fever_(Kylie_Minogue_album)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Virgil_Solis_Febris.jpg/500px-Virgil_Solis_Febris.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/